Yes,

One way I've seen the needed insight expressed is
roughly that

     Despite NetHack having perhaps a hundred times
     as many moves in a typical game as does chess,
     each move in NetHack should take about the same
     amount of time as a move in chess, _on average_
     about 2.5 minutes.

This allows time for carefully reviewing all
messages from the last move, for checking all
elements of the status line and thinking through
their implications (and if the status line is full,
realizing that some warning might be hidden, and
figuring out what precautions you might need to take
for the parts you can't see), identifying each
monster and object in view, and deciding what next
move to make, among sometimes a couple of dozen
choices, based on all this effort.

